[Music]  they came  from across the uk to party illegally  these are the scenes of a lockdown rave  three  thousand people descending on a village  in south wales  causing widespread disruption the party  started in the early hours of sunday  and carried on throughout the bank  holiday weekend  the loud music and swarms of cars have  left the locals furious  50 if not more was going up and down the  street vans  big you know the ones you get did you  put the horses in the back  then because they couldn't go up the top  they were turning around to back down  up down up down all night and what about  the sound of the music  that's it this has turned into what is  now a significant police operation  arrests have been made and the party has  ended  south wales police branded it as totally  unacceptable  and are looking at all pieces of  legislation  in a statement the police federation  which represents officers in england and  wales  said these are already existing officers  which are being pulled away from other  duties to manage those ignorant enough  to ignore the guidance designed to keep  them and others safe  many officers dispersing large  gatherings are often subject to abuse  and violence from the selfish  individuals  who choose to attend these and this is  completely unacceptable  we are not society's punching bags  but it isn't just in wales where  predominantly young people are raving  and breaking the rules  in norfolk police shut down an all-night  unlicensed music event  which hosted more than 500 people in  leeds and essex  thousands of pounds worth of music  equipment were seized by police  which they believe was set to be used  for illegal parties  [Music]  it was just on friday the government  introduced new measures  to clamp down on such illegal gatherings  now  organizers can receive a fine of up to  10  000 pounds those who attend and those  who don't wear face coverings where it's  mandatory  could be given a 100 pound fine  the home secretary lashed out at the  organizers of these parties  reminding them they are not above the  law the kovid 19 pandemic  may have frustrated many with festivals  wiped out  and numerous summer events across the  country cancelled  but it's illegal parties like these that  are the perfect scenario  for the spread of this infectious  disease insomnia rasheed sky news

